                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

What does `lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)` actually do?,Holds the lock for 3000 ms and then releases it automatically,"Waits up to 3000 ms to get the lock, then returns true if it got in or false if the time ran out",Pauses the thread for 3000 ms before trying,Retries every 3000 ms until it eventually succeeds,,2,"The timeout is on WAITING for the lock, not on holding it. Once acquired the thread keeps the lock until it calls unlock, however long that takes.",2

Real code:

public class Counter {

    Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() throws InterruptedException {

        if (lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                count++;
            } catch (Exception e) {

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

A thread reaches the `else` branch, prints its message, and increment() returns. What happened to that increment?,It is queued and applied as soon as the lock is free,"It never happens. The thread gave up and moved on, so this call simply did not do its work - count was not increased",It is retried automatically by the loop in CounterRunnable,It throws an InterruptedException to the caller,,2,"This is the trade-off being demonstrated. Never blocking forever also means the work can silently be skipped, so the caller has to be fine with that.",2

Real code:

public class Counter {

    Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() throws InterruptedException {

        if (lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                count++;
            } catch (Exception e) {

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

The lock is held for 3000 ms (the sleep) and the timeout to acquire it is also 3000 ms. Why does that combination guarantee the else branch is reached?,Because the timeout and the sleep cancel each other out,"Because a waiting thread gives up after 3000 ms, which is exactly as long as the holder keeps the lock - so the waiter runs out of time at roughly the moment the lock is freed and usually loses the race for it",Because tryLock always fails on the second call,Because sleep() releases the lock partway through,,2,"The numbers were chosen deliberately so the failure path is reliably visible. Make the timeout 10000 instead and the waiter would almost always succeed, and the else branch would never be seen.",2

