public class Counter {

    Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() throws InterruptedException {

        if (lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                count++;
            } catch (Exception e) {

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

public class CounterRunnable implements Runnable {

    private final Counter counter;

    public CounterRunnable(Counter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            try {
                counter.increment();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        }
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}

Actual output when this is run:

    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t1
    Final count: 5
    t2

t2 printed its give-up message 4 times out of 5 loop iterations. What does that tell you about what t2 achieved?,t2 failed completely and incremented nothing,"t2 only got the lock once out of five attempts - four of its five increments were skipped, so it contributed 1 to the count instead of 5",t2 incremented 4 times and failed once,t2 was never started properly,,2,"Reading the output as a count of failures is the point: 4 messages means 4 abandoned increments, so the two threads together did 6 increments rather than the 10 the loops asked for.",2

Real code:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Counter counter = new Counter();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(new CounterRunnable(counter), "t1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(new CounterRunnable(counter), "t2");

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        //t2.join();

        System.out.println("Final count: " + counter.getCount());
    }
}

Actual output when this is run:

    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t2 dont care about the lock .....will return later
    t1
    Final count: 5
    t2

The program prints 'Final count: 5', and the line `//t2.join();` is commented out. Running the same code with that join restored prints 6. Why the difference?,Restoring the join causes an extra increment to run,"Without it, main does not wait for t2, so the count is printed while t2 is still working - 5 is simply the value at that instant, not the final value",The join changes the lock timeout,6 is wrong - the correct answer is 5,,2,"Verified both ways: 5 without the join, 6 with it. The printed label says 'Final count' but nothing has made it final, which is exactly what join() is for.",2

