public class CounterRunnable implements Runnable {

    private final Counter counter;

    public CounterRunnable(Counter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            try {
                counter.increment();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        }
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}

Two threads each loop 5 times, so 10 increments were requested. Is a final count below 10 a bug here?,Yes - every increment must eventually happen or the lock is broken,"No. Skipping work when the lock is busy is exactly what tryLock with a timeout was chosen for. It would be a bug in a counter that must be exact, and is fine for work that can be dropped",Yes - the timeout should be removed,"No, because count++ is atomic anyway",,2,Whether this design is right depends entirely on the job. Dropping a metrics sample is fine

Real code:

public class Counter {

    Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() throws InterruptedException {

        if (lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                count++;
            } catch (Exception e) {

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

Why is `lock.unlock()` inside a `finally` block rather than written as the last line of the `try`?,Purely style - both run at the same time,"Because finally runs even if the code inside throws or returns early. As the last line of try, an exception would skip it and the lock would stay held forever",Because unlock() can only be called from a finally block,Because finally runs before the try body,,2,"A lock held forever is worse than the bug that caused it: every other thread then blocks or times out, and the program looks hung rather than broken.",2

Real code:

public class Resource {

    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void outer() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in outer");
            Thread.sleep(10000);
            inner();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void inner() {
        if (lock.tryLock()) {
            try {
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in inner");
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " ignored the lock");
        }
    }
}

In outer(), `lock.lock()` is written OUTSIDE and immediately before the `try`. Why not put it inside the try?,It makes no difference where it goes,"If lock() itself failed and it were inside the try, the finally would still run unlock() - releasing a lock this thread never acquired. Taking the lock first, then entering try, keeps unlock paired with a successful lock",Because lock() cannot be called inside a try block,Because the try block must contain only the shared data access,,2,"The standard shape is: acquire, then try, then unlock in finally. It guarantees unlock runs exactly when a matching lock succeeded.",2

