public class Resource {

    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void outer() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in outer");
            Thread.sleep(10000);
            inner();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void inner() {
        if (lock.tryLock()) {
            try {
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in inner");
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " ignored the lock");
        }
    }
}

Thread t3 calls outer(), which takes the lock and then calls inner(), where `tryLock()` is attempted on the SAME lock. What happens?,"tryLock returns false, because the lock is already held","tryLock returns true. The lock is re-entrant, so a thread that already holds it is allowed to take it again - t3 prints 'acquired lock in inner'",The thread deadlocks against itself and hangs,An IllegalMonitorStateException is thrown,,2,Verified in the output: 't3 acquired lock in inner'. Re-entrancy is what makes it safe for one locked method to call another locked method on the same object.,2

