public class Resource {

    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void outer() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in outer");
            Thread.sleep(10000);
            inner();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void inner() {
        if (lock.tryLock()) {
            try {
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " acquired lock in inner");
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " ignored the lock");
        }
    }
}

Now suppose this lock were NOT re-entrant. What would happen to t3 when outer() calls inner()?,Exactly the same - re-entrancy makes no difference here,"inner()'s tryLock would fail, so t3 would print 'ignored the lock'. Worse, with a plain lock() instead of tryLock the thread would wait for a lock only it can release - stuck on itself forever",The JVM would detect it and throw an error,outer() would release its lock automatically first,,2,"This is the reason re-entrancy exists. Without it, any method holding a lock could never safely call another method that needs the same one.",2

