public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Resource resource = new Resource();

        Thread t3 = new Thread(new OuterRunnable(resource), "t3");   // calls outer()
        Thread t4 = new Thread(new InnerRunnable(resource), "t4");   // calls inner()

        t3.start();
        Thread.sleep(200);
        t4.start();

        t3.join();
        t4.join();
    }
}

Actual output when this is run:

    t3 acquired lock in outer
    t4 trying to get lock in inner
    t4 ignored the lock
    t3 acquired lock in inner

t4 calls inner() directly while t3 holds the lock, and prints 'ignored the lock'. Why did t4 fail where t3 succeeded on the very same method?,Because t4 started later than t3,"Because re-entrancy applies to the thread that already OWNS the lock. t3 owns it, so it gets in again

