Actual output when this is run:

    t3 acquired lock in outer
    t4 trying to get lock in inner
    t4 ignored the lock
    t3 acquired lock in inner

In the output, t4's 'ignored the lock' appears BEFORE t3's 'acquired lock in inner', even though t3 started first. Why?,The output lines are printed in random order,"t3 takes the lock, then sleeps 10 seconds before it ever reaches inner(). t4 starts 200 ms in, is refused straight away, and finishes long before t3 wakes up",t4 has higher priority than t3,t3's message was buffered and printed late,,2,The 10-second sleep inside the locked region is what makes the whole demo observable - it holds the lock long enough for another thread to arrive and be turned away.,2

Real code:

public class Warehouse {

    private int product;
    private boolean hasProduct = false;

    public synchronized void takeProduct() {

        while (!hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Consumer: Taking product " + product);
        hasProduct = false;
        notify();
    }

    public synchronized void putProduct(int product) {

        while (hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        this.product = product;
        hasProduct = true;

        System.out.println("Producer: Putting product " + product);
        notify();
    }
}

takeProduct() is synchronized, so the consumer holds the lock when it calls wait(). What does wait() do to that lock?,Keeps hold of it while the thread waits,"Releases it. The waiting thread gives the lock up so another thread can enter the synchronized method, and only takes it back after being woken",Passes it directly to the producer,"Destroys it, so a new lock must be created",,2,"This is the single most important fact about wait(). Without releasing the lock, the producer could never get into putProduct(), and the program would stop dead.",2

Real code:

