What would happen if wait() or notify() were called from a method that was NOT synchronized?,"It would work, just without protecting the data",IllegalMonitorStateException at runtime - you must hold an object's lock to call wait() or notify() on it,The compiler would reject it,The call would be ignored silently,,2,"wait() has to release a lock, and notify() has to signal about one, so both are meaningless unless the calling thread actually holds it.",2

Real code:

void main() {
    Warehouse warehouse = new Warehouse();

    Thread consumer = new Thread(new Consumer(warehouse));
    Thread producer = new Thread(new Producer(warehouse));

    // Consumer is started FIRST
    consumer.start();

    // Producer is started SECOND
    producer.start();
}

Actual output (first few lines):

    Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...
    Producer: Putting product 1
    Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...
    Consumer: Taking product 1
    Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...
    Producer: Putting product 2
    Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...
    Consumer: Taking product 2

The consumer is deliberately started FIRST. What does the output show as a result, and why is this a good way to start the demo?,"The consumer takes a product before any exists, printing a wrong value","The first line is 'Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...' - the consumer arrives, finds nothing, and waits. It shows straight away that the consumer correctly does nothing rather than reading an empty warehouse",The consumer throws an exception because the warehouse is empty,"The producer is forced to run first anyway, so the order makes no difference",,2,Starting the consumer first sets up the harder case on purpose. If it had run second there would always be a product waiting and the waiting path would never be exercised.,2

Real code:

public class Warehouse {

    private int product;
    private boolean hasProduct = false;

    public synchronized void takeProduct() {

        while (!hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Consumer: Taking product " + product);
        hasProduct = false;
        notify();
    }

    public synchronized void putProduct(int product) {

        while (hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        this.product = product;
        hasProduct = true;

        System.out.println("Producer: Putting product " + product);
        notify();
    }
}

Actual output (first few lines):

    Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...
    Producer: Putting product 1
    Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...
    Consumer: Taking product 1
    Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...
    Producer: Putting product 2
    Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...
    Consumer: Taking product 2

The output strictly alternates: put 1, take 1, put 2, take 2, and so on. Which part of Warehouse forces that, given both loops run 5 times independently?,"The synchronized keyword, which makes the methods run in turn","The hasProduct flag together with the two while loops - the producer cannot put while it is true and the consumer cannot take while it is false, so neither can run twice in a row","The notify() calls, which alternate between the two threads",The fact that both loops happen to run 5 times,,2,"synchronized alone would only stop them overlapping, not force them to alternate. The alternation comes from the condition each side waits on, since the warehouse holds exactly one item.",2

