            }
        }

        this.product = product;
        hasProduct = true;

        System.out.println("Producer: Putting product " + product);
        notify();
    }
}

Why is the boolean hasProduct needed at all, instead of just checking the product value?,It is not needed - checking whether product is 0 would work,"Because the product value alone cannot say whether the warehouse is full or empty. After a product is taken the number is still sitting in the field, so a separate flag is what records that it has already gone",Because booleans are faster to read than ints,Because product is private and cannot be read inside the class,,2,Note takeProduct() never clears product - it only sets hasProduct to false. The flag is the real state

Real code:

public class Counter {

    Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() throws InterruptedException {

        if (lock.tryLock(3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                count++;
            } catch (Exception e) {

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " dont care about the lock .....will return later");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

public class Warehouse {

    private int product;
    private boolean hasProduct = false;

    public synchronized void takeProduct() {

        while (!hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Consumer: Warehouse is empty. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Consumer: Taking product " + product);
        hasProduct = false;
        notify();
    }

    public synchronized void putProduct(int product) {

        while (hasProduct) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Producer: Warehouse is full. Waiting...");
                wait();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        this.product = product;
        hasProduct = true;

        System.out.println("Producer: Putting product " + product);
        notify();
    }
}

Both classes hold a lock while doing something slow - Counter sleeps 3 seconds inside the lock, the Warehouse waits inside a synchronized method. Why is one a problem and the other not?,Both are equally bad and should be rewritten,"Counter genuinely keeps the lock during its sleep, blocking everyone else - fine for a demo, bad practice in general. The Warehouse's wait() releases the lock, so it is not holding anything while it waits","Both are fine, because each has a timeout","The Warehouse is worse, because a synchronized method locks for longer",,2,Time spent inside a synchronized region only hurts if the lock is actually being held. That is precisely what separates sleeping from waiting.,2
